lane: stm-fifo-post-cas
why: okayStrongChunk 128.0 vs zioStrongChunk 125.9 is the last lane
  behind. Profile: Tuple2 23%, receiveManyAsync's loop 17%,
  List.reverse 13%, Queue.dequeue 8%. The cause is that the bulk take
  rebuilds the persistent queue PER ELEMENT inside one transaction --
  4000 intermediate states nobody will ever see -- and does it BEFORE
  the CAS, so a losing transaction pays for all of it too.
how: a purpose-built persistent Fifo inside StmChannel (two lists plus
  a size), and move the O(k) work into the action, which transact
  already runs only after the CAS wins. Whole-buffer take becomes an
  O(1) state transition; the chunk is filled after the win, reading
  `out` forward into ascending indices and `in` forward into
  DESCENDING ones -- so FIFO order falls out and no reversed list is
  ever materialised. Safe because the old lists are immutable and the
  CAS already made the take exclusive.
  Inherent and staying: one State per transaction (2%), and the
  amortized reverse on the elementwise path when `out` empties.
